1
00:00:00,883 --> 00:00:10,264
Welcome to Japan's prefectures, part 2 of 4. First, I clear the chosen names
for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10,919 --> 00:00:22,007
We work through 8 of the Japan regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:22,672 --> 00:00:25,099
Let's bring Niigata into view.

4
00:00:29,067 --> 00:00:39,133
Niigata faces the Sea of Japan on the west of central Honshū, below Yamagata
and Fukushima and above Toyama, with the city of Niigata on the shore.

5
00:00:39,785 --> 00:00:57,142
The Shinano, which runs the length of the prefecture, is Japan’s longest river
at 367 kilometres, and the Koshihikari rice grown on its plains is the
standard by which Japanese rice is judged.

6
00:00:57,802 --> 00:01:05,941
Remember the new lagoon where the Shinano changed course, with more sake
breweries than any prefecture in the country.

7
00:01:06,612 --> 00:01:08,864
Let's bring Ishikawa into view.

8
00:01:13,114 --> 00:01:23,215
Ishikawa faces the Sea of Japan on the west of central Honshū, with Toyama
above and Fukui and Gifu below, and the city of Kanazawa on the shore.

9
00:01:23,866 --> 00:01:36,277
Its gold leaf is 99% of Japan’s supply, beaten thin enough to lay on
lacquerware and temples, and, occasionally, ice cream.

10
00:01:36,935 --> 00:01:44,528
Remember the stone river, the great garden of Kenrokuen, and the sheets of
gold thin enough to wrap a sweet.

11
00:01:45,215 --> 00:01:47,340
Let's bring Tochigi into view.

12
00:01:52,796 --> 00:02:05,544
Tochigi is inland in the north of the Kantō plain, north of Tokyo, between
Gunma and Ibaraki, with the city of Utsunomiya in its middle and Fukushima
above.

13
00:02:06,197 --> 00:02:15,276
Nikko in it holds the mausoleum of Tokugawa Ieyasu, the most ornate building
complex in Japan and a UNESCO world heritage site.

14
00:02:15,939 --> 00:02:23,184
Remember the horse chestnut tree that gave the name, and the gilded mausoleum
of the shogun in the mountains.

15
00:02:24,004 --> 00:02:25,920
Let's bring Toyama into view.

16
00:02:35,190 --> 00:02:45,384
Toyama faces the Sea of Japan on the west of central Honshū, with Niigata
above and Ishikawa below, and the city of Toyama on the bay.

17
00:02:46,042 --> 00:02:58,151
The Tateyama range rises 3,000 metres almost straight out of that bay, and the
snow corridor at Murodō is dug through drifts up to 20 metres high every
spring.

18
00:02:58,816 --> 00:03:06,665
Remember the wall of mountains coming straight out of the sea, and the
travelling medicine sellers who left a box on the doorstep.

19
00:03:07,607 --> 00:03:10,115
Let's bring Gunma into view.

20
00:03:21,181 --> 00:03:30,353
Gunma is inland in the north-west of the Kantō plain, with the city of
Maebashi at the foot of Mount Haruna and Tochigi above it and Saitama below.

21
00:03:31,023 --> 00:03:43,132
The Tomioka silk mill here, opened in 1872 with French machinery,
industrialised Japanese silk and is now a UNESCO world heritage site.

22
00:03:43,798 --> 00:03:52,540
Remember the old name meant a cart, and the hot spring at Kusatsu whose water
is strong enough to dissolve a nail.

23
00:03:53,569 --> 00:03:55,694
Let's bring Ibaraki into view.

24
00:04:07,005 --> 00:04:16,769
Ibaraki faces the Pacific on the north-east of the Kantō plain, north of
Chiba, with the city of Mito near the shore and Gunma and Tochigi inland
behind it.

25
00:04:17,429 --> 00:04:24,929
It makes most of Japan’s nattō, and Tsukuba in it is a science city built from
nothing in the 1960s.

26
00:04:25,591 --> 00:04:31,559
Remember the thorn castle of the old legend, and the garden of three thousand
plum trees above the bay.

27
00:04:32,319 --> 00:04:34,490
Let's bring Nagano into view.

28
00:04:44,696 --> 00:04:54,890
Nagano is inland in the middle of Honshū, a long valley ringed by the
mountains of the Japanese Alps, with the city of Nagano in its northern half.

29
00:04:55,542 --> 00:05:07,013
The Zenkōji temple here is older than the split of Japanese Buddhism into
sects, so it belongs to none and is run jointly by two schools, Tendai and
Jōdo.

30
00:05:07,666 --> 00:05:14,528
Remember the monkeys that soak in a hot spring in the snow, and the temple
that belongs to no sect at all.

31
00:05:15,317 --> 00:05:17,697
Let's bring Fukui into view.

32
00:05:28,439 --> 00:05:39,143
Fukui faces the Sea of Japan on the west of central Honshū, with Ishikawa
above and Shiga and Kyōto below, and the city of Fukui a little way inland.

33
00:05:39,798 --> 00:05:47,553
More dinosaur fossils have been found here than anywhere else in Japan, and
several of the new species carry the name Fukui.

34
00:05:48,216 --> 00:05:56,146
Remember the province of dinosaurs, and the spectacle frames that nearly every
pair of glasses in the country is made from.

35
00:06:05,788 --> 00:06:19,140
8 regions of Japan, one for each target of the map: Niigata, Ishikawa,
Tochigi, Toyama, Gunma, Ibaraki, Nagano and Fukui.

36
00:06:19,818 --> 00:06:30,139
Remember the shape of Japan from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
